// 🔹 throw — Keyword
// throw keyword is used to create an exception object manually i.e., normally method creates an exception object as exception occurs in that method, but when we use throw, programmer is responsible to create an exception object.
// Throw keyword is used in case of Unchecked Exception.
// We can throw only single Exception.
// Throw keyword is used within the method.
// throw keyword is followed by new instance.
// We cannot write any statement after throw keyword (it will throw unreachable statement)and thus it can be used to break the statement.
// Example Code:-

public class YoungAgeException extends RuntimeException
{
   YoungAgeException(String msg)
   {
       super(msg);
   }
}

class Test
{
   public static void main(String[] var0)
   {
      int age = 16;
      if(age<18)
      {
         throw new YoungAgeException("min age should be 18");
      }
      else
      {
         System.out.println("Great");
      }
   }
}

